# Claims — {{PROJECT_NAME}}

**What this project might be able to claim, and how much of it is earned.** Read
at the start of every session. Cap: {{CLAIMS_CAP}} lines. An index — the
reasoning behind each row lives in `{{CLAIMS_DIR}}/`, one file per claim.

`{{FINDINGS_FILE}}` records what this project *learned*; `{{PRIOR_WORK_FILE}}`
records what was *already known*. This records what the two together would let
us **assert** — and, more usefully, what they do not.

**Novelty** `✗` searched and absent · `~` adjacent work exists · `=` anticipated
· `?` **unsearched, a liability**
**Evidence** `●●` measured here · `●` partial or elsewhere · `○` asserted

Links: `F` = {{FORGE_URL}}

---

## Why the two axes are separate columns

A claim can be wide open in the literature and completely unevidenced by us.
That combination *feels* like a contribution and is the weakest square on the
board. Collapsing novelty and evidence into one "confidence" score is how a
project talks itself into a claim it cannot defend, so they never share a column.

## Format

```
| # | Claim | Nov | Ev | Detail |
|---|---|:-:|:-:|---|
| 1 | <claim, stated so a hostile reader can attack it> | `✗` | `●●` | <number> · [`abc1234`](F/commit/abc1234) · [reading](<research doc>) |
```

**An evidence cell cites a commit. A novelty cell cites what was searched** — a
paper, or the section of a prior-work document. A verdict with no citation is a
recollection, and a recollection is indistinguishable from a measurement once it
is in a table.

## Rules

- **A row moves only with a commit, and the commit goes in the cell.** An
  unbacked promotion here is worse than one in `{{FINDINGS_FILE}}`, because this
  is the file a write-up gets built from.
- **`?` is a liability, not a blank claim of novelty.** It means nobody has
  checked. Searching can only lower it.
- **Never promote on the novelty axis because the evidence axis moved.**
  Measuring something more carefully does not make it newer.
- **A conjunction inherits the MINIMUM of its parts.** If the headline claim is a
  chain, score each link and give the chain the weakest link's marks — never the
  strongest.
- **Record the evidence *against*** as deliberately as the evidence for. A
  register that lists only what might be new, while the counter-evidence sits
  unindexed in a long document, is a machine for self-persuasion.
- **A row gets a file in `{{CLAIMS_DIR}}/` when it outgrows its cell** — and that
  file never copies this table back. Two copies drift, and the drift is
  invisible.

## When it grows past the cap

Do not delete. Move the reasoning into `{{CLAIMS_DIR}}/<id>-<slug>.md` and leave
the row pointing at it. The cap is what keeps this a thing that gets read.

